/-!
# RUBIK'S CUBE (n × n × n, Slice Turn Metric) is NP-complete

An `n × n × n` Rubik's Cube consists of `n³` unit cubies; every cubie face on the exterior of
the cube carries a sticker of one of six colours. The cube's volume is subdivided into `n`
slices normal to each of the three axes, and in the Slice Turn Metric a move is a rotation of a
single slice by any multiple of `90°` (Demaine, Eisenstat and Rudoy, Section 2.2). The puzzle is
solved when each face is monochromatic in a different colour. Given a configuration and a
number `k`, the question is whether the configuration can be solved in at most `k` moves
(Problem 2). A configuration is the result of applying a sequence of moves to the canonical
solved cube `C₀` of Section 2.4 (`t(C₀)` for `t` in the group generated by the slice turns).

Coordinates: cubies have coordinates in `Fin n` along the axes `0, 1, 2` (`x, y, z`). A sticker
is named by the outward normal of its face, an axis `a` and a sign `s` (`true` for `+`), and by
the coordinates `u` and `v` of its cubie along the axes `a + 1` and `a + 2` (mod 3); its cubie
has coordinate `n - 1` (for `+`) or `0` (for `-`) along `a`. The quarter turn about axis `b`
maps the coordinates `(p, q)` along `(b + 1, b + 2)`, centred at the cube's centre, to
`(-q, p)`; in `Fin n` coordinates, negation is `Fin.rev`. A move `(b, j, t)` turns the slice of
cubies with coordinate `j` along `b` by `t + 1` quarter turns (`90°`, `180°` or `270°`). It is
stated by pulling colours back: the sticker at position `x` of the slice receives the colour
that was at `x` turned `t + 1` times, so the slice turns by `-(t + 1)` quarter turns; since `t`
ranges over all three nonzero amounts, the set of moves is the same.

The same paper proves the Slice Quarter Turn Metric version (moves of `±90°` only)
NP-complete as well; it is a different problem and is not stated here.

Encoding: the instance is decoded from a bitstring with the repository's
canonical `MetaMathlibExt.BitstringEncoding`. Undecodable bitstrings, and
bitstrings decoding to malformed instances, are no-instances.

NP-completeness is stated in the same form as the proved results in
`CSLibExt.Computability.Complexity.Karp`: membership in
`MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP`, plus a polynomial-time many-one
reduction from every language in `NP`
(`Cslib.CookLevin.PolyManyOneRed`).
-/

/-- **RUBIK'S CUBE (n × n × n, Slice Turn Metric) is NP-complete.** Source: Demaine,
Eisenstat and Rudoy (2018), Problem 2. -/
public theorem_wanted rubiksCube_npComplete :
    rubiksCubeDec ∈ MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP ∧
      ∀ L : MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.DecisionProblem,
        L ∈ MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP →
          Cslib.CookLevin.PolyManyOneRed L rubiksCubeDec

end MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.RubiksCube
